import LibcMath.Extracted.Expf
import LibcMath.IR.InterpLemmas

/-!
# The main computation of `expf` as a Lean function

On the arguments whose bit patterns lie in `[0x32800001, 0x42B20000)` or
`[0xB2800001, 0xC2CFF1B5)` the extracted `expf` runs one fixed sequence of floating-point
operations (blocks 15 to 21), except at `0xC236BD8C`, which has a block of its own (block 1).
`expfMain` is that sequence written with FloatLib's operations directly:

* `k` is `128 * x` rounded to an integer (`expfNearestInt`), by adding and subtracting `2 ^ 23`
  and correcting the result by one where the difference to `128 * x` exceeds one half;
* `r = x - k / 128` (`expfReduce`);
* with `n` the integer `k`, the table entries are `expM1[(n + 0x3400) >>> 7]`, which is
  `exp ((n >>> 7) - 104)`, and `expM2[n &&& 0x7F]`, which is `exp ((n &&& 0x7F) / 128)`;
* the result is `expM1[…] * expM2[…] * p r` in binary64, converted to binary32, where `p` is a
  polynomial of degree four (`expfPoly`).

The interpreter does not continue with a NaN that an arithmetic instruction produced, and it has
no result when `fptosi` fails or a table index is out of bounds. `expfMain` answers `none` in the
same cases (`IR.nonNaN`, `IR.fptosi?`, `Array.getElem?`), in the order in which the interpreter
meets them, so that the two can be compared as they stand. `expfStructured` adds the result at
`0xC236BD8C`.
-/

namespace LibcMath.Extracted

